                %ul
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#remarks_2"} Remarks
              %li
                %a{:href => "#remarks_3"} Remarks
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            the construction of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+space"} loop space
            of a given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            is familiar.
          %p
            This construction may be generalized to any other
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            and in fact to any other
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pullbacks"} homotopy pullbacks
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Loop space objects are defined in any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{C}
            with
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pullbacks"} homotopy pullbacks
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+object"} pointed object
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{C}
            with point
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {*} \to X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+space+object"} loop space object
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+the"} the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pullback"} homotopy pullback
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega X
            of this point along itself:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Ω
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\Omega X &amp;\to&amp; {*}
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  {*} &amp;\to&amp; X
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            A (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalised+element"}> generalised
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+point"} element
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega X
            may be thought of as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop"} loop
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            at the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+point"} base point
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
          %p
            When the point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x : {*} \to X
            is not clear from context, we can write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi x
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega_x X
            or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega(X,x)
            to indicate the point.
          %h3#remarks Remarks
          %p
            Since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{C}(X,-)
            commutes with homotopy limits, one has a natural homotopy equivalence
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Ω
                      %mover
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi y
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega_{\bar{y}}\mathbf{C}(X,Y)\simeq \mathbf{C}(X,\Omega_y Y)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and pointed object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Y,y)
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{C}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{y}
            denotes the morphism
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mo *
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to * \to Y
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration+sequence"} fibration sequence
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-category
          %h2#explicit_constructions Explicit constructions
          %p
            Usually the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            in question is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} presented
            by concrete 1-categorical data, such as that of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pullback"} homotopy pullback
            has various realizations as an ordinary
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
          %p
            Notably it may be expressed using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+objects"} path objects
            which may come from
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+objects"} interval objects
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+category"} homotopical category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+object"} interval object
            may still exist and may be used as indicated in the following to construct loop space objects.
          %h3#FreeLoopSpaceObject Free loop space objects
          %p
            In a category with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+object"} interval object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                  %mi I
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mn 1
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} * \xrightarrow{0} I \xleftarrow{1} *
            the
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+loop+space+object"} free loop space object
            is the part of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+object"} path object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mi I
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi I
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^I = [I,B]
            which consists of closed paths, namely the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
